<section class="section" aria-labelledby="line-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="line-title">Where the line sits</h2>
    </header>
    <section class="data-table-wrap" tabindex="0" aria-label="Audit support and audit compared, scrolls sideways on small screens">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Point</th><th scope="col">Audit support</th><th scope="col">The audit itself</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Purpose</th><td>Get your records ready</td><td>Give an independent opinion or report</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Who</th><td>Accountant, bookkeeper or an outside firm</td><td>For a company's statutory audit, a Chartered Accountant under section 141 of the Companies Act. A tax audit needs a CA in practice</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Whose side</th><td>Works for you</td><td>Must remain independent of you</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">What you get</th><td>Schedules, reconciliations and a tidy file</td><td>A signed audit report</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Can they be the same person?</th><td colspan="2">Do not assume so. An auditor preparing your books can raise independence issues. Ask your auditor before arranging this.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>
    <p class="section-foot">Do not accept a "signed audit" from someone who has not shown you a membership number and certificate of practice. See {% routeLink "/guides/accountant-vs-chartered-accountant/", "how to check a CA's credentials" %}.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section section--tint" aria-labelledby="ready-title">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="section-head">
      <h2 id="ready-title">Audit readiness checklist</h2>
      <p>Ask your auditor for their own list. This is a general starting point, and it does not cover everything.</p>
    </header>
    <div class="deliver-grid">
      <article><h3>Books closed</h3><p>Trial balance finalised, with suspense and unexplained items cleared.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Bank</h3><p>Reconciliations for every account, with year-end statements or balance confirmations.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Parties</h3><p>Ledgers for customers and suppliers, with confirmations for large balances.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Stock</h3><p>Count sheets signed by the person counting, and how it was valued.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Fixed assets</h3><p>Register, invoices for additions, sale records and depreciation working.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Taxes</h3><p>GST returns matched with the books, TDS returns and challans, and any notices received.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Payroll</h3><p>Salary registers, and PF and ESI records where applicable.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Loans and contracts</h3><p>Sanction letters, statements and key agreements.</p></article>
      <article><h3>Company records</h3><p>Minutes, statutory registers and past filings, if you are a company.</p></article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" aria-labelledby="need-title">
  <div class="wrap layout-side">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="need-title" class="first-h2">Do you need an audit at all?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Companies:</strong> secondary sources report that statutory audit applies regardless of size. A company generally needs an auditor from its first year.</li>
        <li><strong>Tax audit:</strong> under section 63 of the Income-tax Act, 2025, generally where business turnover exceeds ₹1 crore (₹10 crore with low cash dealings) or professional receipts exceed ₹50 lakh. See {% routeLink "/services/income-tax-return-filing/", "income tax returns" %}.</li>
        <li><strong>LLPs and others:</strong> depends on size and rules. Ask a professional.</li>
        <li><strong>Others who may ask:</strong> a bank, investor or regulator may need audited accounts or specific certificates.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="good">What good support looks like</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A clear list of what is missing, before the auditor arrives</li>
        <li>Schedules that tie directly to the trial balance</li>
        <li>A single organised file, indexed for the auditor</li>
        <li>Honest flags on anything unusual, rather than something hidden</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Be cautious of anyone who offers to "adjust" figures to get through an audit. Directors and proprietors remain responsible for the accounts.</p>

      <h2 id="ask">Questions to ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>What will you prepare, and what stays with me?</li>
        <li>Have you supported audits for my kind of business?</li>
        <li>Are you independent from my auditor, or connected with them?</li>
        <li>How do you handle items you cannot explain?</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fees">What affects the fee</h2>
      <p>State of the books, volume, stock, how many schedules are needed, and any backlog. Audit fees are separate, and are agreed with the auditor, who for a statutory audit must be a practising Chartered Accountant.</p>

